  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale  \n   We propose a convex relaxation approach combined with a two-phase optimization strategy. First, we solve a relaxed convex problem to find a good initial solution, then refine it using projected gradient descent. This leverages the structure of the problem to escape local optima while maintaining feasibility.\n\n2. Mathematical specification  \n   We formulate the objective as minimizing the maximum overlap:\n   \n   \\[\n   C_5 = \\max_{k} \\sum_{i} h[i](1 - h[(i+k) \\mod n]) \\cdot dx\n   \\]\n   \n   Subject to \\(0 \\leq h[i] \\leq 1\\) and \\(\\sum h[i] = n\\_points / 2\\). The problem is non-convex due to the \\(h[i]h[(i+k) \\mod n]\\) terms. To simplify, we first solve a relaxed version where we minimize the sum of overlaps, then project onto the original constraints.\n\n3. Implementation procedure  \n   a. Initialize using `initial_h_values`, ensuring feasibility.  \n   b. First phase: Solve a convex relaxation using CVXPY to minimize total overlap.  \n   c. Second phase: Apply projected gradient descent on the refined solution from phase b, computing gradients via finite differences.  \n   d. After each update, clip \\(h[i]\\) to [0,1] and rescale to maintain the integral constraint.  \n   e. Track the best solution and terminate if no improvement occurs for 50 iterations.\n\n4. Search choices and useful variations  \n   Use \\(n\\_points = 100\\) for speed. Set learning rate to 0.01, decrease by 10% every 100 iterations. Clip velocities to ensure stability.\n\n5. Compute budget and robustness  \n   The method is budget-friendly, with each iteration taking ~0.1s. Expect 1000 iterations within 600s. Include progress checks and fallback to initial solution if needed.\n\n6. Validation and return contract  \n   Ensure \\(h[i] \\in [0,1]\\) and sum to \\(n\\_points / 2\\). Validate final solution using the exact evaluator. Return the best h and its C\u2085 value.",
